[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-94082"] :question/problem "Find the center of the elliptic cone $2(x+2)^{2}+3(y-1)^{2}={z}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of an elliptic cone with center at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=\\frac{(z-{z}_{0})^{2}}{{c}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the center, we rewrite our equation in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+2)^{2}+3(y-1)^{2} & =(z-0)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{1/2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{1/3} & =(z-0)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-2))^{2}}{1/2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{1/3} & =(z-0)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic cone that has its center at $(-2,1,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-94081"] :question/problem "Given the elliptic cone $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{4}=(z+3)^{2},$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercepts of the elliptic cone, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{4} & =(0+3)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{4}{4} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+1 & =9 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2} & =8 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =-2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(-2,0,0)$ and $(6,0,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-94602/selection/1f3e79b8aee9d024fcb5caa3a9de812f9540a13cbc2233484f68e46661092a07" :answer/id #uuid "f40b0875-c4e9-5c16-be1d-714a22fd5efc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,0,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94602/selection/9733f343a615e3416853edcc9201d7d7c52a4030ae82318824dde2e0022c6a10" :answer/id #uuid "51cc2545-a80f-5f6a-871e-8d96e17107be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,0,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94602/selection/bebefd592b4bd526b503d9b72bb520da84e97e3db514c97335eaca1bf3336991" :answer/id #uuid "278f55ed-3618-5b17-8d5d-04c2004a54e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-7,-3,-21)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94602/selection/758500018f7fa700254d4b7d713ccf88cc979f5e6869053b9cf4ed8aa9a00f53" :answer/id #uuid "1b11e0dc-71d9-52b9-8411-70368204ad26" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(7,3,21)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94602/selection/113180091eb788d8f4e2735d1f8b58c3f1e385d11ef4d7ca4ed41bfe164d4b62" :answer/id #uuid "e5126299-745f-523f-bdd1-47c5d8a8602c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,7,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94602/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4bee53b8-fbfb-54f8-84e2-98f951401745" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94602/selection/1f3e79b8aee9d024fcb5caa3a9de812f9540a13cbc2233484f68e46661092a07" :answer-field/choices ["q-94602/selection/1f3e79b8aee9d024fcb5caa3a9de812f9540a13cbc2233484f68e46661092a07" "q-94602/selection/9733f343a615e3416853edcc9201d7d7c52a4030ae82318824dde2e0022c6a10" "q-94602/selection/bebefd592b4bd526b503d9b72bb520da84e97e3db514c97335eaca1bf3336991" "q-94602/selection/758500018f7fa700254d4b7d713ccf88cc979f5e6869053b9cf4ed8aa9a00f53" "q-94602/selection/113180091eb788d8f4e2735d1f8b58c3f1e385d11ef4d7ca4ed41bfe164d4b62"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94602" :question/id #uuid "b3ea4837-32ba-5546-9a0a-236b390a5978" :question/math-academy-id "q-94602" :question/problem "Find the center of the elliptic cone $7{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=21{z}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of an elliptic cone with center at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=\\frac{(z-{z}_{0})^{2}}{{c}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the center, we rewrite our equation in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(x-0)^{2}+3(y-0)^{2} & =21(z-0)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x-0)^{2}}{1/7}+\\frac{(y-0)^{2}}{1/3} & =\\frac{(z-0)^{2}}{1/21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic cone that has its center at $(0,0,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94602/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e0b4012-403b-5739-8f33-3c79111129fd"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94602"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94603/selection/a43a88d369550433276a114bbe7524a7124a2a4c741b9afb2ae110442ae78d8b" :answer/id #uuid "f79f5dde-c4d1-5102-8735-fceb7c96e88a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94603/selection/8ac686da95423c0c09dfdf791c62d91afd64c47ce7b97e917fe5fd4edf8e40ce" :answer/id #uuid "15ef40c7-5f0b-546e-b664-3a99e6e8bbd6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,1.5,0)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94603/selection/1747ee16795748150eac86cae5ae5149f084c64e610d396740e2ebf54073efee" :answer/id #uuid "5aaa03a3-b6df-5703-9a5c-25aabe31d37c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(0,4,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94603/selection/4c414ef65dada8c45a3ee6ed06053325f148f5573095e36040a752863266ad5f" :answer/id #uuid "36c58996-a72f-5721-89f0-9ee7a56e5440" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,1.5,0)$ and $(0,4,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94603/selection/69b74a649318f29621b7468d7664dc52bfc9c58b73434c672027b589072da849" :answer/id #uuid "f5b4bae7-eccb-5f5e-8cc3-fbd7f2ec53c6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,2,0)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94603/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4e90a7eb-3ef4-5e98-95d4-9c571fd3c2f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94603/selection/a43a88d369550433276a114bbe7524a7124a2a4c741b9afb2ae110442ae78d8b" :answer-field/choices ["q-94603/selection/a43a88d369550433276a114bbe7524a7124a2a4c741b9afb2ae110442ae78d8b" "q-94603/selection/8ac686da95423c0c09dfdf791c62d91afd64c47ce7b97e917fe5fd4edf8e40ce" "q-94603/selection/1747ee16795748150eac86cae5ae5149f084c64e610d396740e2ebf54073efee" "q-94603/selection/4c414ef65dada8c45a3ee6ed06053325f148f5573095e36040a752863266ad5f" "q-94603/selection/69b74a649318f29621b7468d7664dc52bfc9c58b73434c672027b589072da849"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94603" :question/id #uuid "61c83505-bb91-59ab-a450-ae05b1d9fb7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-94603" :question/problem "Given the elliptic cone $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2},$ find its intercepts with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercepts of the elliptic cone, we plug $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{0}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2} \\\\ 0+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercept with the $y$-axis is $(0,0,0)$ only." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-94603/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e0b4012-403b-5739-8f33-3c79111129fd"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94603"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-176833/selection/297c9a95c84cbe9d386db850c8bac52ac6f1461dcf46890f66d6f4944e7ab746" :answer/id #uuid "8231b6c6-cfa5-5a9f-84b4-4fe2c7413ced" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,-2,0)$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-176833/selection/6a1a9ac37cc9aeedec82a65daefc79b4c3ebe340dfdb7d8753348549b4de4585" :answer/id #uuid "0125cce2-e928-5bca-85fb-b39ef96df5a7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,-8,0)$ and $(0,7,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-176833/selection/8